154
00:11:10,088 --> 00:11:13,914
we looked at encoder-decoder models, right? Primarily, uh, we looked at why

155
00:11:14,414 --> 00:11:17,824
they are needed, right? So we looked at the aspect that, okay, encoder actually encodes

156
00:11:18,324 --> 00:11:21,750
an input sequence to learn a latent or a hidden representation for the entire sequence,

157
00:11:21,767 --> 00:11:24,859
which is then passed along to a decoder, which can then use it to generate

158
00:11:25,277 --> 00:11:28,732
an output sequence in an autoregressive manner, just like what any

159
00:11:29,232 --> 00:11:32,283
language model will do. And we saw why this is needed, because there were many

160
00:11:32,783 --> 00:11:36,707
applications where such kind of an architecture could be useful. Applications which

161
00:11:37,207 --> 00:11:41,392
are like machine translation, code generation, image captioning, summarization. A key insight

162
00:11:41,892 --> 00:11:44,760
about these applications was that their input and output properties are different. That's why you

163
00:11:45,260 --> 00:11:48,506
need different models to actually first encode them

164
00:11:48,554 --> 00:11:51,157
and then decode them in a different format. Right.

165
00:11:51,799 --> 00:11:55,089
We also looked at the aspect of

166
00:11:55,105 --> 00:11:58,282
bidirectionality in these encoder-decoder models

167
00:11:58,459 --> 00:12:02,085
because they actually then can be used. So encoder can be used in a way

168
00:12:02,585 --> 00:12:05,744
that it can actually model sequence from both sides, thus giving

169
00:12:05,856 --> 00:12:08,552
it more capacity and a better way of modeling sequences.

170
00:12:09,050 --> 00:12:12,114
But then we also looked at why the decoder needs to be unidirectional

171
00:12:12,614 --> 00:12:15,885
because autoregressive models cannot know the future. Right. And we

172
00:12:16,046 --> 00:12:19,385
also looked at key challenges of these encoder-decoder model is that because

173
00:12:19,401 --> 00:12:22,951
they need paired data. For machine translation, you need paired

174
00:12:23,451 --> 00:12:26,710
data across languages. For image captioning, you need paired image-text data, and so on

175
00:12:27,210 --> 00:12:30,260
and so forth. And then I kind of warned you that the reason this is

176
00:12:30,760 --> 00:12:34,243
challenging is not just that you just need paired data, but also because

177
00:12:34,420 --> 00:12:37,938
finding this paired data in the real world is

178
00:12:38,050 --> 00:12:41,648
really hard, right? So that's a major

179
00:12:42,148 --> 00:12:45,963
challenge, right? Then we also would— we looked at is that there is major limitation

180
00:12:46,075 --> 00:12:50,482
in these encoder-decoder models is because this green block

181
00:12:50,982 --> 00:12:54,969
here, which is the signal hidden state, just crams the entire input

182
00:12:55,469 --> 00:12:59,104
sequence into a single vector. We also looked at the nice swear

183
00:12:59,120 --> 00:13:00,834
words from a very famous NLP professor,

184
00:13:02,164 --> 00:13:05,129
and that was a major limitation from this model.

185
00:13:05,850 --> 00:13:09,023
And to fix this, what we looked at is an

186
00:13:09,580 --> 00:13:13,052
attention-based encoder-decoder model where the idea was

187
00:13:13,149 --> 00:13:16,508
to, instead of just using this one hidden

188
00:13:16,556 --> 00:13:21,811
representation, you can actually attend to the associations

189
00:13:21,875 --> 00:13:25,273
between different encoder states and the decoder state at a specific

190
00:13:25,338 --> 00:13:28,360
point in time so that you can actually model these representations better.

191
00:13:29,957 --> 00:13:33,638
So the outcome was literally here that we were able to fix the temporal bottleneck

192
00:13:33,946 --> 00:13:38,154
because there was a direct link between decoder and encoder. States. And we also

193
00:13:38,219 --> 00:13:42,172
got interpretability based on how the attention weights actually give

194
00:13:42,672 --> 00:13:46,980
us some more insights about what these models are actually learning. Then we

195
00:13:47,060 --> 00:13:50,771
also looked at a specific issue with recurrent encoders, right? So encoder-decoder

196
00:13:51,271 --> 00:13:54,434
models not only had this limitation, which could have been fixed with just adding attention

197
00:13:54,707 --> 00:13:59,066
to the weights, but come on. But there

198
00:13:59,566 --> 00:14:03,417
are also other issues with these encoder— in the encoder part

199
00:14:03,917 --> 00:14:07,156
of these encoder-decoder models, one which could fix with adding attention

200
00:14:07,189 --> 00:14:10,812
to the encoder-decoder association. But the other was that

201
00:14:11,312 --> 00:14:15,195
the hidden states of an encoder were still computed in series, which was the temporal—

202
00:14:15,276 --> 00:14:18,307
like the time complexity bottleneck. But the other aspect was that these

203
00:14:18,323 --> 00:14:22,464
were still based on an RNN or an LSTM, where vanishing gradients

204
00:14:22,593 --> 00:14:27,995
and long-range dependencies seems to be a problem. And to actually get

205
00:14:28,495 --> 00:14:30,867
rid of these issues, we said that, OK, a simple idea that we can apply

206
00:14:30,915 --> 00:14:33,948
from when we looked at attentive encoder-decoder models,

207
00:14:34,061 --> 00:14:37,825
we can apply the same idea by changing attention between

208
00:14:38,325 --> 00:14:41,889
encoder-decoder to attention on the encoder itself, which we also called self-attention,

209
00:14:42,403 --> 00:14:46,243
which then basically allows us to ditch the sequential computation

210
00:14:46,275 --> 00:14:50,099
in an RNN, but also allows us to get rid of the issues

211
00:14:50,276 --> 00:14:53,120
of long-range dependencies because literally now we have a direct link,

212
00:14:53,264 --> 00:14:57,104
but we are actually capturing pairwise links between all encoder

213
00:14:57,604 --> 00:15:01,281
hidden states. Okay. And then with this idea, we actually looked at

214
00:15:01,781 --> 00:15:04,986
the full transformer. Which was made of both an encoder and a decoder.

215
00:15:05,291 --> 00:15:09,249
Right. And we also studied this architecture very, very carefully.

216
00:15:09,298 --> 00:15:12,503
We looked at different parts of this architecture and

217
00:15:13,625 --> 00:15:16,991
we basically looked at primarily the key concepts of

218
00:15:17,491 --> 00:15:21,511
being the multi-headed attention concept, which actually just powers the whole transformer.

219
00:15:21,895 --> 00:15:25,501
But then we also looked at what are the differences between the encoder

220
00:15:26,001 --> 00:15:29,028
architecture and the decoder architecture. And we saw,

221
00:15:29,733 --> 00:15:33,662
you know, why you need masked multi-headed attention in the decoder and

222
00:15:33,742 --> 00:15:36,501
why you need the cross-attention part in the decoder, which then allows us to,

223
00:15:36,566 --> 00:15:40,143
you know, transformer to fully model the sequence

224
00:15:40,175 --> 00:15:43,673
in a better way, right? And then,

225
00:15:44,090 --> 00:15:48,004
you know, there was something that we kind of said that it is required

226
00:15:48,036 --> 00:15:51,133
because we said that, okay, modern transformers actually

